IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `fbtaa-employee`
IMPORT `fbtaa-formulas`

-- Fringe Benefits Tax Assessment Act 1986, ss 10, 11, 12, 135P, 135Q, 136(1) -- worked cases for fbtaa-formulas.l4
-- (24 October 2026).
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Expected results were worked by hand from
-- the text and the formulas transcribed in ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A03280.formulas.md, before the encoding was run.
-- Every figure is hypothetical. The statutory interest rate of 8% is ASSUMED (REQ-0110: the RBA rate is not deposited). The
-- rate of FBT is the deposited Fringe Benefits Tax Act's: 47%, or 49% for the years starting 1 April 2015 and 2016.

§ `s 135Q -- employees of s 57A and s 58 employers`

-- A hospital nurse with $5,300 of exempt benefits (quasi), none taxable, 2026: 5,300 > 2,000; 5,300 / 0.53 = 10,000.
#ASSERT `My reportable fringe benefits amount from a s 57A or s 58 employer` 0 5300 2026 EQUALS `answered` 10000
-- $1,000 + $1,000 = 2,000, not more than $2,000: nil. $500 + $1,620 = 2,120: 2,120 / 0.53 = 4,000.
#ASSERT `My reportable fringe benefits amount from a s 57A or s 58 employer` 1000 1000 2026 EQUALS `answered` 0
#ASSERT `My reportable fringe benefits amount from a s 57A or s 58 employer` 500 1620 2026 EQUALS `answered` 4000
-- The year starting 1 April 2016, at 49%: 5,100 / 0.51 = 10,000. A year before 2015: the rate is not deposited.
#ASSERT `My reportable fringe benefits amount from a s 57A or s 58 employer` 0 5100 2016 EQUALS `answered` 10000
#ASSERT `My reportable fringe benefits amount from a s 57A or s 58 employer` 0 5300 2014 EQUALS `not covered` `the deposited Fringe Benefits Tax Act gives the rate only for years of tax from 1 April 2015`
-- Any employer: a s 57A employer goes to s 135Q (10,000); any other to s 135P (5,300 of benefits, also 10,000).
#ASSERT `My reportable fringe benefits amount, any employer` 0 5300 2026 TRUE EQUALS `answered` 10000
#ASSERT `My reportable fringe benefits amount, any employer` 5300 0 2026 FALSE EQUALS `answered` 10000
-- The employer's mirror gives the same.
#ASSERT `As the employer, the reportable amount, any employer` 0 5300 2026 TRUE EQUALS `answered` 10000

§ `ss 11, 12 -- depreciation and interest of an owned car`

-- B = 200% / 8 years = 0.25.
#ASSERT `the depreciation rate B` 200 8 EQUALS 0.25
-- Bought for $40,000 on 1 April 2025 (365 days owned in the FBT year starting 2025): at 1 April 2026, 40,000 - 40,000 x 0.25 =
-- 30,000. Bought 1 April 2024: 40,000 x 0.75 x 0.75 = 22,500 at 1 April 2026.
#ASSERT `The depreciated value of the car at the start of the year` 40000 2025 365 2026 200 8 EQUALS 30000
#ASSERT `The depreciated value of the car at the start of the year` 40000 2024 365 2026 200 8 EQUALS 22500
-- Bought 1 October 2025, 182 days owned in that FBT year (of 365): 40,000 x (1 - 0.25 x 182 / 365) = 12,780,000 / 365.
#ASSERT `The depreciated value of the car at the start of the year` 40000 2025 182 2026 200 8 EQUALS 12780000 DIVIDED BY 365

§ `s 136(1) -- business use percentage`

#ASSERT `The business use percentage of my car` 6000 20000 EQUALS 30

§ `s 10 -- the cost basis`

-- Owned, FBT year 2026 (365 days), depreciated value 30,000 at its start, owned all year: depreciation 30,000 x 0.25 = 7,500;
-- interest 30,000 x 8% = 2,400; with $5,000 of car expenses and $1,600 of registration and insurance: 16,500.
#ASSERT `The operating cost of the car` `owned` 5000 1600 0 30000 365 2026 200 8 8 EQUALS 16500
-- Leased: 5,000 + 1,600 + 9,000 of lease charges = 15,600.
#ASSERT `The operating cost of the car` `leased` 5000 1600 9000 0 365 2026 200 8 8 EQUALS 15600
-- Bought during 2026 for $40,000, owned 182 days: depreciation 40,000 x 0.25 x 182 / 365 = 1,820,000 / 365; interest
-- 40,000 x 0.08 x 182 / 365 = 582,400 / 365; operating cost 6,600 + 2,402,400 / 365.
#ASSERT `The operating cost of the car` `owned` 5000 1600 0 40000 182 2026 200 8 8 EQUALS 6600 PLUS (2402400 DIVIDED BY 365)
-- s 10(2) on 16,500 at 30% business use, $1,000 paid: 16,500 x 70% - 1,000 = 10,550. No log book: BP nil, 15,500. A payment
-- above the private part: nil.
#ASSERT `The cost basis taxable value of my car` 16500 TRUE 30 1000 EQUALS 10550
#ASSERT `The cost basis taxable value of my car` 16500 FALSE 30 1000 EQUALS 15500
#ASSERT `The cost basis taxable value of my car` 16500 TRUE 30 20000 EQUALS 0
-- s 10(5): the statutory formula for the same car (cost $40,000, first held 1 April 2025, all 365 days, $1,000 paid) is
-- 40,000 x 0.2 - 1,000 = 7,000. The cost basis 10,550 exceeds it, so 7,000 applies. At 80% business use the cost basis is
-- 16,500 x 20% - 1,000 = 2,300, below 7,000, so 2,300.
#ASSERT `The taxable value of my car under the cost basis election` 16500 TRUE 30 1000 40000 (Date 1 4 2025) 2026 0 365 EQUALS 7000
#ASSERT `The taxable value of my car under the cost basis election` 16500 TRUE 80 1000 40000 (Date 1 4 2025) 2026 0 365 EQUALS 2300
#ASSERT `As the employer, the taxable value of the car under the cost basis` 16500 TRUE 80 1000 40000 (Date 1 4 2025) 2026 0 365 EQUALS 2300
